# dd-dev's fish configuration, baked into the image at ~/.config/fish/config.fish
# (Dockerfile.dev). fish is the dev account's login shell, and this is the other
# half of that.
#
# /workspace — the bind-mounted host clone — is where the work is, so a shell that
# starts anywhere else moves there. The shells that start anywhere else are the
# LOGIN ones: sshd chdirs to the home directory before exec'ing the shell, so
# `ssh -p 2222 dev@<pi>` and Zed's remote shell land in /home/dev no matter what the
# image's WORKDIR says. A `docker exec` shell already starts in /workspace (WORKDIR)
# and the cd costs it nothing.
#
# Guarded on `status is-login` rather than run unconditionally, because
# `fish -c ...` from a script has to keep the directory its caller chose — a config
# that cd'd every fish would move the ground under anything scripted.
# Guarded on the directory too: a container brought up with the bind mount missing
# should still hand you a shell to find that out from, not an error on every login.
#
# **The corollary, and it bites:** `ssh <host> <command>` is NOT a login shell. sshd
# execs the shell with argv[0] as `fish`, not `-fish`, so this cd does not run and the
# command inherits $HOME as its cwd. That matters for exactly one command — the abduco
# one, because abduco takes the session's cwd from whoever creates it, and `-A` then
# hands that same cwd to everybody who attaches later. A `claude` session rooted at
# /home/dev is a claude in the wrong project, and it would outlive the mistake.
# So every ssh one-liner in this repo spells its own `cd /workspace` (Makefile
# `dev-claude`, docs/pi_dev_setup.md, the entrypoint banner) rather than relying on
# this file. Keep it that way if you add another.
if status is-login
    if test -d /workspace
        cd /workspace
    end
end
